---

### Java Program: Custom Linked List with Add and Remove Methods

In this program, we define an interface `LinkedListOperations` that outlines the basic operations for a linked list. A class `CustomLinkedList` implements this interface and provides the functionality for adding and removing elements. The implementation uses a singly linked list structure, where each node contains a data element and a reference to the next node in the sequence.

The `village` of Java programming is rich with data structures, and implementing a linked list from scratch is a great way to understand how they work under the hood. This example will help you grasp the fundamentals of dynamic memory allocation and pointer-like behavior in Java.

---

### Interface Definition

```java
public interface LinkedListOperations<T> {
    void addAtBeginning(T data);
    void addAtEnd(T data);
    void remove(T data);
    void display();
}
```

This interface defines four methods:
- `addAtBeginning(T data)`: Adds a new element at the start of the list.
- `addAtEnd(T data)`: Adds a new element at the end of the list.
- `remove(T data)`: Removes the first occurrence of the specified element.
- `display()`: Prints all elements in the list.

---

### Node Class

```java
public class Node<T> {
    T data;
    Node<T> next;

    public Node(T data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

The `Node` class is a generic class that holds the data and a reference to the next node. It is used to build the linked list.

---

### Custom Linked List Implementation

```java
public class CustomLinkedList<T> implements LinkedListOperations<T> {
    private Node<T> head;

    public CustomLinkedList() {
        this.head = null;
    }

    @Override
    public void addAtBeginning(T data) {
        Node<T> newNode = new Node<>(data);
        newNode.next = head;
        head = newNode;
    }

    @Override
    public void addAtEnd(T data) {
        Node<T> newNode = new Node<>(data);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
            return;
        }
        Node<T> current = head;
        while (current.next != null) {
            current = current.next;
        }
        current.next = newNode;
    }

    @Override
    public void remove(T data) {
        if (head == null) return;

        if (head.data.equals(data)) {
            head = head.next;
            return;
        }

        Node<T> current = head;
        while (current.next != null && !current.next.data.equals(data)) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        if (current.next != null) {
            current.next = current.next.next;
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void display() {
        Node<T> current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.print(current.data + " -> ");
            current = current.next;
        }
        System.out.println("null");
    }
}
```

---

### Main Class to Test the Implementation

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CustomLinkedList<String> list = new CustomLinkedList<>();

        list.addAtBeginning("Apple");
        list.addAtEnd("Banana");
        list.addAtBeginning("Cherry");
        list.addAtEnd("Date");

        System.out.println("Initial List:");
        list.display();

        list.remove("Banana");

        System.out.println("After removing 'Banana':");
        list.display();
    }
}
```

---

### Explanation of the Code

- The `CustomLinkedList` class maintains a reference to the first node (`head`).
- When adding at the beginning, a new node is created and its `next` pointer is set to the current `head`. Then, the `head` is updated to point to the new node.
- When adding at the end, the program traverses the list until it finds the last node and appends the new node there.
- The `remove` method checks if the element to be removed is at the head. If not, it traverses the list and updates the `next` pointer of the previous node to skip the node to be removed.
- The `display` method prints the entire list in order.

---

### Output of the Program

```
Initial List:
Cherry -> Apple -> Banana -> Date -> null
After removing 'Banana':
Cherry -> Apple -> Date -> null
```

---

### Conclusion

This Java program demonstrates a basic implementation of a singly linked list with methods for adding and removing elements. It uses an interface to define the contract and a class to implement the functionality. The program is generic, allowing it to work with any data type. Understanding such implementations is crucial for mastering data structures and algorithms in Java.

By building and testing this program, you gain hands-on experience with linked lists, which are fundamental in many real-world applications such as memory management, file systems, and more.

